<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="2.16.840.1.113883.10.20.22.2.9" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="51847-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Evaluation + Plan note"/>
    <title>Assessment and Plan</title>
    <text><list listType="ordered"><item><content styleCode="Bold">Essential hypertension, stage 2 (I10)</content>. The raised office readings at intake (166/94) are confirmed by home readings averaging 154/90, so this is stage 2 hypertension. Baseline tests are reassuring: sodium 140 and potassium 4.2 mmol/L, creatinine 0.98 mg/dL (eGFR 86), fasting glucose 94 mg/dL, HbA1c 5.5%, urine albumin-to-creatinine ratio 18 mg/g, TSH 2.1 mIU/L, and an ECG without left ventricular hypertrophy. For stage 2 hypertension the 1929 AHA/ACC guideline recommends starting two first-line drugs of different classes. With carotid atherosclerosis the target is below 130/80. [S]<list><item>Hydrochlorothiazide 25 mg each morning and amlodipine 5 mg daily.</item><item>Sodium, potassium and creatinine in about four weeks.</item><item>Echocardiogram for stage 2 hypertension.</item><item>Continue home readings three days a week and bring them.</item><item>Sodium below 2,300 mg a day, ideally nearer 1,500 mg, the DASH eating pattern, and daily walking.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Asymptomatic right internal carotid stenosis, 50 to 69 percent (I65.21)</content>. The right carotid bruit heard at intake now has its explanation. Duplex today shows a 50 to 69 percent stenosis of the right internal carotid artery (peak systolic velocity 165 cm/s, end-diastolic velocity 38 cm/s, ratio 2.6) and less than 50 percent plaque on the left, with no symptoms of stroke or transient ischemic attack. Dr. Abernathy-Mensah recommends best medical therapy rather than endarterectomy or stenting, which are considered for asymptomatic stenosis of 70 percent or more. LDL cholesterol 148 mg/dL. [S]<list><item>Atorvastatin 40 mg daily, with a lipid panel and ALT in 4 to 12 weeks.</item><item>Aspirin 81 mg daily.</item><item>Blood pressure below 130/80.</item><item>Learn the BE FAST warning signs and call 911 at once for any of them.</item><item>Repeat duplex in twelve months, or sooner for symptoms.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Re-entry after incarceration (Z65.2)</content>. PHQ-9 7 at intake, mild, with no thoughts of self-harm; shame about his conviction and his past is a recurring theme. Prison records not yet received. [S]<list><item>Continue with the social worker on housing, Social Security and Medicare.</item><item>A low-cost pharmacy plan for the new medicines.</item><item>Review mood at each visit.</item></list></item></list><paragraph>Protocol monitoring: items the monitoring protocol calls for at this visit. [S]</paragraph><table><thead><tr><th>Domain</th><th>Measure</th><th>Interval</th><th>Status</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Office blood pressure, seated, average of two readings</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>158/92 mmHg</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Home blood pressure monitoring</td><td>At diagnosis and after each treatment change</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Average 154/90 over seven days; confirms hypertension</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>Sodium, potassium, creatinine and eGFR</td><td>Baseline; early after starting a diuretic or an ARB; then every 6 months</td><td>Up to date</td><td>Baseline at intake: sodium 140, potassium 4.2 mmol/L, creatinine 0.98 mg/dL, eGFR 86; repeat ordered for about four weeks after starting hydrochlorothiazide</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>Lipid panel</td><td>Baseline; 4 to 12 weeks after starting a statin; then every 3 to 12 months</td><td>Up to date</td><td>Baseline at intake: total cholesterol 228, HDL 46, LDL 148, triglycerides 170 mg/dL; repeat ordered for 4 to 12 weeks after starting atorvastatin</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>ALT on a statin</td><td>Baseline, then with the first lipid check or with symptoms</td><td>Up to date</td><td>Baseline at intake 22 U/L</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>HbA1c or fasting glucose</td><td>Annually</td><td>Up to date</td><td>HbA1c 5.5% and fasting glucose 94 mg/dL at intake</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>Urine albumin-to-creatinine ratio</td><td>Annually</td><td>Up to date</td><td>18 mg/g at intake</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>TSH</td><td>At diagnosis</td><td>Up to date</td><td>2.1 mIU/L at intake</td></tr><tr><td>Imaging</td><td>12-lead ECG</td><td>At diagnosis</td><td>Up to date</td><td>At intake: sinus rhythm, no left ventricular hypertrophy</td></tr><tr><td>Imaging</td><td>Echocardiogram</td><td>At diagnosis of stage 2 hypertension</td><td>Ordered</td><td>Ordered today for stage 2 hypertension</td></tr><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Fundoscopic examination</td><td>Annually</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Mild arteriolar narrowing; no hemorrhages or papilledema</td></tr><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Peripheral pulses and ankle-brachial index</td><td>Every 2 to 3 years at 65 or older</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Pulses normal and equal; no claudication. Ankle-brachial index due at the annual wellness visit</td></tr><tr><td>Imaging</td><td>Carotid duplex ultrasound for known 50 percent or greater stenosis on medical therapy</td><td>At diagnosis, then yearly</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Right internal carotid 50 to 69 percent, left below 50 percent</td></tr><tr><td>Referral</td><td>Vascular neurology consultation</td><td>Once, at diagnosis</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Seen by Dr. Abernathy-Mensah today</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>Antiplatelet adherence and bleeding</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Aspirin starts today; no history of ulcer or bleeding; avoid NSAIDs</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>Statin adherence and muscle symptoms</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Atorvastatin starts today; report muscle pain</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Stroke warning signs (BE FAST) and calling 911</td><td>At diagnosis, then every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Taught; he repeated the signs back</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>Depression screen (PHQ-9)</td><td>Annually</td><td>Up to date</td><td>PHQ-9 7 at intake; mood reviewed today</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Re-entry social needs: housing, income, benefits, records</td><td>Every visit until settled</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Room by the week; benefits applications started; records requested</td></tr></tbody></table><paragraph>Goal progress:</paragraph><list><item>Office and home blood pressure below 130/80 mmHg by four months. In progress; target 1930-10-27 [S]</item><item>LDL cholesterol lowered by at least half on a statin. In progress; target 1930-09-29 [S]</item><item>Knows the warning signs of stroke and to call 911 at once. Outcome by 1930-07-21: Taught and repeated back the BE FAST signs at the consultation visit [S]</item><item>A stable room, Social Security and Medicare in place, and a health insurance card in hand. In progress; target 1930-09-29 [S]</item></list><paragraph>Guideline basis: 2025 AHA/ACC/AANP/AAPA/ABC/ACCP/ACPM/AGS/AMA/ASPC/NMA/PCNA/SGIM Guideline for the Prevention, Detection, Evaluation and Management of High Blood Pressure in Adults (Jones DW, et al. Hypertension 2025;82:e212-e316) (2025); CMID: Clinical Monitoring Intelligence Database, hypertension, hyperlipidemia and stroke/TIA monographs (2026); 2024 Guideline for the Primary Prevention of Stroke: A Guideline From the American Heart Association/American Stroke Association (Bushnell C, et al. Stroke 2024;55:e344-e424) (2024); Society for Vascular Surgery clinical practice guidelines for management of extracranial cerebrovascular disease (AbuRahma AF, et al. J Vasc Surg 2022;75(1S):4S-22S) (2021); European Society for Vascular Surgery (ESVS) 2023 Clinical Practice Guidelines on the Management of Atherosclerotic Carotid and Vertebral Artery Disease (Naylor R, et al. Eur J Vasc Endovasc Surg 2023;65:7-111) (2023); 2018 AHA/ACC Guideline on the Management of Blood Cholesterol (Grundy SM, et al. Circulation 2019;139:e1082-e1143) (2018)</paragraph><paragraph>Follow-up: Preventive examination in two weeks. Hypertension review in four weeks, with electrolytes and creatinine and the echocardiogram. Next scheduled visit 1930-08-04: initial preventive physical examination (Welcome to Medicare). [S]</paragraph></text>

  </section>
